2. secret.yaml
A Secret stores sensitive values, such as passwords, tokens, and keys.

Example:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: app-secret
type: Opaque
data:
  DB_USERNAME: YWRtaW4=
  DB_PASSWORD: cGFzc3dvcmQ=

The values in data are typically Base64-encoded.
For example:  admin
becomes:  YWRtaW4=


Base64 encoding is just converting the original text into another format.
For example:  admin
becomes:  YWRtaW4=
And:  password
becomes:  cGFzc3dvcmQ=


So in your Secret:
data:
  DB_USERNAME: YWRtaW4=
  DB_PASSWORD: cGFzc3dvcmQ=

Kubernetes stores the values in Base64 format.
⚠️ Base64 is not encryption. Anyone who gets the Secret can decode it back to admin and password.


If you mean who converts the Base64 value back to the original value, then:
Kubernetes decodes the Base64 value when the Secret is used by a Pod.
Kubernetes does it when it provides the Secret to the Pod.

Example:
data:
  DB_USERNAME: YWRtaW4=
Kubernetes stores:  YWRtaW4=
When the Pod uses this Secret, Kubernetes makes the value available as:   admin

So you don't need to manually decode it for your application.
However, if a person gets access to the Secret, they can manually decode it too, because Base64 is not encryption:
echo "YWRtaW4=" | base64 --decode
Result:  admin
So:  You → encode → Kubernetes Secret → Kubernetes provides original value → Pod/application.



Suppose your real username and password are:
admin
password

Step 1 — You know the original values

You want Kubernetes to use:
Username = admin
Password = password

Step 2 — If you use data:, you cannot put them directly
You convert them to Base64:
admin     → YWRtaW4=
password  → cGFzc3dvcmQ=

Then your file becomes:
data:
  DB_USERNAME: YWRtaW4=
  DB_PASSWORD: cGFzc3dvcmQ=

Step 3 — Kubernetes knows these are encoded
Kubernetes reads:  YWRtaW4=
and understands the actual value is:   admin

So:
YOU
 ↓
original value: admin
 ↓
encode to Base64
 ↓
Secret YAML: YWRtaW4=
 ↓
Kubernetes
 ↓
application gets: admin

